# Cloudflare Pages response headers. Replaces the next.config.ts headers() block,
# which static export can't emit. Same baseline security headers, applied to
# every path: clickjacking, MIME sniffing, and referrer leakage. (No CSP — see
# the note in the original next.config.ts: the inline theme-init script would
# need a per-request nonce/hash and a misconfigured CSP failing closed is a worse
# risk than its marginal benefit here.)
#
# Pages also adds `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` to every static asset by
# default. It is detached from every path: the pages and the files they load
# (compare-data.json, Next's page data) carry the same stock data as the API,
# and no other site needs to read them from a browser. Links, images and
# server-side fetches don't use CORS and are unaffected.
/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  ! Access-Control-Allow-Origin

# The service worker must always be revalidated so a fresh build's worker is
# picked up promptly — a cached sw.js would strand installed users on an old
# version (the in-app "newer snapshot" prompt still depends on this fetch).
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Public data API: /api/v1/* (static files generated by scripts/gen-api.mjs) and
# the bulk availability feed at the site root, /availability.json (read by
# muster.fusionspace.co). Cached ~10 min; the data refreshes on the ~hourly
# deploy, which also invalidates the edge copy.
#
# Browser access is decided per request by the Pages Functions in
# functions/api/v1/_middleware.ts and functions/availability.json.ts (rule in
# lib/apiAccess.ts): fusionspace.co sites and the origins in API_ALLOWED_ORIGINS
# get Access-Control-Allow-Origin with their own origin, other origins get 403.
# Those Functions serve the file through context.next(), which still applies the
# rules in this file, so Cache-Control below holds.
#
# Pages adds `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` to every static asset by default;
# the `!` lines detach it from the API files, so a browser on another site can't
# read them even when the Functions don't run (for example if the daily
# Functions allowance runs out and the project fails open). Never set it here;
# lib/apiAccess.test.ts fails if a rule does, or if a `!` line goes missing.
/availability.json
  ! Access-Control-Allow-Origin
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=600

/api/*
  ! Access-Control-Allow-Origin
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=600
